On Mon, Dec 10, 2001 at 12:49:49PM -0800, Tom Dropka wrote:
> BTW, I saw the same unaligned accesses with the stock RH 2.4.3-12
> kernel, which is why I tried 2.4.16.

If you have the driver compiled as module (which is probably the
case for stock RH kernel), you can try to fix it even without
recompiling - pass "rx_copybreak=1518" option to the module.
Or try appended patch.

> > Don Becker's drivers, when "adjusted" for Alpha, force a copy of the
> > data from the "raw" buffer to another buffer where the data will be
> > appropriately aligned, thus avoiding any unaligned accesses at the
> > price of the packet copy.

Yes - it's a common problem on alpha and several other platforms
with network cards capable to DMA only on a 32-bit boundary (via-rhine
is one of those). Without copying the packet to another buffer IP header
will be misaligned, and you'll have tons of unaligned traps in the
TCP/IP layer.

Ivan.

--- linux/drivers/net/via-rhine.c.orig	Sat Nov 10 00:45:36 2001
+++ linux/drivers/net/via-rhine.c	Tue Dec 11 13:11:33 2001
@@ -87,7 +87,12 @@ static int max_interrupt_work = 20;
 
 /* Set the copy breakpoint for the copy-only-tiny-frames scheme.
    Setting to > 1518 effectively disables this feature. */
-static int rx_copybreak;
+#if defined(__alpha__) || defined(__sparc__) || defined(__mips__) || \
+    defined(__arm__)
+static int rx_copybreak = 1518;
+#else
+static int rx_copybreak = 200;
+#endif
 
 /* Used to pass the media type, etc.
    Both 'options[]' and 'full_duplex[]' should exist for driver




