We use "make boot"  or "make bootpfile" depending if we are going to
have the kernel local or served from a bootp server..

Originally (2.0 kernel days) I used aboot to create bootp images, but
the "make bootpfile" in the kernel build is a better place, since if
your working with bootp, you do not need aboot. And you do not need an
extra build environment (aboot) to process the kernel image into a
bootp(able) image.

you can issue a bootp load request from the SRM console, and do not need
aboot involved at all.

JJ

