On Wed, Dec 12, 2001 at 02:24:13PM +0100, Falk Hueffner wrote:
> "Jemiolo, John" <John.Jemiolo@compaq.com> writes:
> 
> > We use "make boot" or "make bootpfile" depending if we are going to
> > have the kernel local or served from a bootp server..
> > 
> > Originally (2.0 kernel days) I used aboot to create bootp images,
> > but the "make bootpfile" in the kernel build is a better place,
> > since if your working with bootp, you do not need aboot. And you do
> > not need an extra build environment (aboot) to process the kernel
> > image into a bootp(able) image.
> 
> IHMO it is much more convenient to have a small utility installed
> rather than a complete unpacked linux source.

Yes, but it is an *additional* requirement.

> Also, this allows making bootp files of kernels you don't have the
> source for. Of course this is just a matter of taste...

But how often does this situation really occur?

Regardless, I don't believe it is Will's intention to replace/delete
the "bootpfile" option in the kernel Makefiles. That option has its
place, and any attempt to remove it would encounter fierce opposition
from yours truly.

However, having a BOOTP-file target under "aboot" is also a useful
thing, in at least two instances I can think of:

1. it allows the BOOTP image to be compressed, saving a considerable
   amount of storage,

2. IIRC, it allows the use of INITRD images.

There's a place for each method of BOOTP image construction, and they
should be able to happily coexist.

--Jay++

