Monday AM, this machine crashed into SRM. I did not write down the console
messages (big mistake). On power recycle, I get 6 beeps (memory problem)
and no console. I attempted the "Fails Safe Boot" (set switch, prepare
floppy) and the machine did its correct beeping and read the diskette, but
still no console (file name changed, as per manual instructions -
DP264SRM.ROM?). I plugged a serial terminal into port 1 (following the
external numbering 1, 2) and tried 9600, 19.2, 38.4 settings. Assuming SRM
was running, I tried various SRM commands on the PC keyboard, including
"set console serial". (I even pulled the graphics card, unplugged the
keyboard and reset the machine, hoping this would force use of the serial
port.)

Not the slightest joy!

The company from which I bought the machine no longer handles alphas and
the machine is no longer under any warranty.

Experienced local alpha administrators have looked at this a little bit
and after their Norwegean-speak, "Yup, that's not good"  come up with
about the only thing I think of -- pull pieces one at a time and test them
in another machine. We can't test a lot of the pieces, however. (I am a
Sociologist who got the grant to purcahse this. I'm OK with Linux and with
basic installation and assembly.)

Thank you for any suggestions.


-- 
