With regards to your message at 07:40 PM 12/20/01, Kurt A. Ludwig. Where 
you stated:
>You could solder them to the original slotB, but you wont gain any speed
>advantage cuz...
>1. Unsupported clock mult... 667 works and 833 works (on a UP2000+)
>2. You'd need an SROM that supported it... which doesn't exist
>3. The chipset needs to support that CPU speed, which it doesnt
>
>fastest you could run the thing safely is 667MHz which you already have.



Actually you could clock it to 750, but that will probably run the same 
performance or worse.
why? Because at 750 it is running a 10X multiplier on a 75MHz front side 
bus (memory) speed.
At least on 667 and 833 it is a multiplier of 83MHz, so the memory is 
clocked faster.
That is why the 750's that were sold all had bigger (8mb) cache.



