On Thu, 20 Dec 2001, Thomas Leitner wrote:
> 
> I'm currently running a UP2000 with a single 666MHz 21264A CPU. Now a
> dealer offered me two 733MHz 21264A barebone CPUs, which raises the
> following questions:

I've seen many of these 733MHz CPUs floating around but I don't know where
they came from. As far as I know the only machines that actually used
733MHz CPUs are the GS (aka: wildfire) series of machines. Remember that
there is no such thing as a socketed EV6 (at least in a production
board).
 
> a.) Can the CPU in a UP2000 CPU-cardridge be swapped by another 21264A CPU?
>     Or is the cardridge sealed somehow? I've never tried to open it.

Well, you could in theory remove your current CPU and replace it with one
of these, but you'd have to be really good with the soldering iron!

> b.) Where can I get an empty UP2000 CPU-cardridge? Are they sold
>     anyway or do they always come along *with* a CPU?

You can't.

> c.) Is there any chance that I can sell the old 666MHz 21264A CPU ?

Well, I don't doubt that there's somebody gullible enough out there to buy
it.

A few things you might want to keep in mind. The UP2000 board only has
SROM code for 667-(2MB/4MB) and 750-8MB so you'd have to run it at 667,
same as
your current CPU - no advantage there. Even if you could somehow get it to
run at 750 it would overheat as there is a heatsink difference between the
667 and 750 SlotBs.

In other words....don't waste your money, and I'd make the same
recommendation to anyone looking at these CPU from a dealer and/or on
ebey.com etc....

--rdp


-- 
Rich Payne
rpayne@alphalinux.org			www.alphalinux.org




