On Sun, Dec 30, 2001 at 04:06:47PM -0800, Brian Kurotsuchi wrote:
> 
> In any case, here's the fdisk output:
> [root@prozac /root]# fdisk -l /dev/hda
> 
> Disk /dev/hda: 12 heads, 55 sectors, 1010 cylinders
> Units = cylinders of 660 * 512 bytes
> 
> 
> 3 partitions:
> #       start       end      size     fstype   [fsize bsize   cpg]
>   c:        1      1010      1010     unused        0     0       
> 

Well, ok, but where are other two partitions - presumably with data?  An
output you qoute says "3 partitions" but you show only one.  Assuming
that they are correct, and this _includes layout, and that you have space
for a boot block, with your partition 'c' you would need a command
like that:

  swriteboot -f3 /dev/hda /boot/bootlx

_provided_ /boot/bootlx is really your boot file.  Installer will NOT
write a boot block automatically with a partition table which contain
a partition like the one above as it is not "smart" enough to know
that "unused" type is safe for the purpose.

If you did not put on this disk any other partitions but 'c', which 
means that there are no filesystems and/or data, then it is kind of
difficult to boot something like that. :-)


  Michal




